block content
    h1 Eye Floaters
    p Definition by #[a(href="https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/eye-floaters/symptoms-causes/syc-20372346") Mayo Clinic]:
    blockquote Eye floaters are spots in your vision. They may look to you like black or gray specks, strings, or cobwebs. They may drift about when you move your eyes. Floaters appear to dart away when you try to look at them directly. Most eye floaters are caused by age-related changes that occur as the jelly-like substance (vitreous) inside your eyes liquifies and contracts. Scattered clumps of collagen fibers form within the vitreous and can cast tiny shadows on your retina. The shadows you see are called floaters.

    p https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t7VxmoMbD-4&list=TLPQMjkxMDIwMjM-_M8jxuL1Fw&index=5&ab_channel=Dr.EricBergDC